course: everyday
lesson: 489
title: Hiding it from friends: being skint at school
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In Nottingham, Jolene's mates all have the new trainers, and her family is skint (has no spare money). So every day for a week Jolene says hers are «in the wash». By Friday she's exhausted: one fib needs another fib, and another. True friends don't care much about trainers. You don't owe anyone an explanation, but you don't need a lie either: «I've got these ones» is enough.
explain: Lots of kids feel ashamed of home. In Liverpool, Reece lives with his nan in a small house with a ripped sofa, so he never invites his mate Jory back. But friends mostly come for the fun, not the furniture. When he finally does, Jory says, «Boss, your nan makes the best chips» («boss» is Scouse for great). In Doncaster someone shouts, «That's Keeley's old coat, I remember it!» and Lacey snaps, «Mind your own, mardy» («mardy» means moody and grumpy). Hand-me-downs are normal and sensible; a shrug, «Yeah, she gave it me, it's warm», takes the sting out.
explain: Some kids are cruel about money. In London a boy says to Precious, «Saw your mum at the food bank, ha!» and a few kids laugh. Precious wants to cry and run; instead she fires back, «So? Your mum shops at Poundland, bruv.» That's understandable, but teasing back can make it worse. Bullying about money is still bullying, and a teacher must deal with it. In Belfast, a girl says, «My dad says people on benefits are lazy.» Megan's mum gets benefits, money from the government, because she looks after Megan's disabled brother all day. Megan could say calmly, «My mum cares for my brother. That's a full-time job.»
explain: School can make money show without meaning to. After summer, the teacher asks, «Where did everyone go?» Hands go up: Spain, Florida, Cornwall (by the sea in England). Ruaridh in Glasgow stays quiet; they've no car and went nowhere. But the park, the library and a day at the beach are a summer too. In Ohio, a teacher hands Everett a note in front of everyone saying his family owes $12 for school lunches, and he stuffs it in his bag. It's not his fault; he should give it to his mom and ask the counsellor about the free lunch form. In Birmingham, Sami's dad says, «Never tell people our business,» but the teacher asks if he has a computer for homework. He can tell just what he needs: «No computer at home.» Schools can lend laptops.
explain: Kindness doesn't need money. In Melbourne, Tasha can't afford a birthday present and makes a friendship bracelet; the birthday girl wears it all day. In Toronto, Jamal's buddy pays for his hot chocolate at Timmies (Canadian for Tim Hortons café), and Jamal, who can't pay back, starts avoiding him. But friends don't keep a bill. A thank-you and being a good mate is enough, and next time Jamal can pay back with a favour, a drawing or a laugh.
fact: Hiding being poor can be exhausting, because one lie usually needs more lies.
fact: You don't owe anyone an explanation about money, and you don't need a lie: «I've got these ones» is enough.
fact: Friends mostly care about having fun together, not about the sofa or the size of the house.
fact: Hand-me-downs are normal and sensible; pointing them out in public is rude.
fact: Teasing about money is bullying, and schools must deal with it; tell a teacher rather than tease back.
fact: Benefits are money from the government for people who can't work, don't earn enough, or care for someone full-time; most people on benefits are not lazy.
fact: Lots of children don't go away in the holidays; days at the park, the library or the beach are a summer too.
fact: A note about unpaid school lunch money is the family's business, not the child's fault; free lunch forms can help.
fact: A child can tell a teacher just what they need, like «no computer at home», without telling all the family's business; schools can lend laptops.
fact: Homemade presents show care and time, and most kids remember who was kind, not what the present cost.
fact: Friends sometimes pay for each other; you can pay back with a favour, a drawing or being a good mate.
fact: Words in this lesson: «skint» means having no money, «boss» (Liverpool) means great, «mardy» (Midlands, North) means moody and grumpy, «bruv» (London) means mate, «Timmies» is Canadian for the Tim Hortons café.
exercise: Why did Jolene keep saying her trainers were «in the wash»? | To hide that her family couldn't buy new ones | Because she really did wash them every day | Because her mum said trainers must be clean | Because she lost them at the park and didn't want to say
exercise: What happened to Jolene by the end of the week? | She was tired of keeping the story going | Her mates bought her a brand-new pair | Her trainers shrank in the washing machine | She got a prize for the cleanest trainers
exercise: What could Jolene say instead of a fib? | «I've got these ones» | «My trainers are at my nan's house in Spain» | «New ones are rubbish, everyone knows that» | «Mine are coming by post, they're gold ones»
exercise: Why won't Reece invite Jory back? | His house is small and the sofa's ripped | His nan doesn't like any of his friends | Jory lives too far away to come round | Jory was rude to him at school last week
exercise: «Boss, your nan makes the best chips.» What does «boss» mean here? | Great | In charge | Too salty | Bossy
exercise: Someone says Lacey's coat was Keeley's. What's a calm way to answer? | «Yeah, she gave it me, it's warm» | «It's not, I bought it in Paris» | Push them into the hedge and run off laughing | Cry and never wear it again
exercise: «Mind your own, mardy.» What does «mardy» mean? | Moody and grumpy | Mad and funny | Messy, smelly and dirty | Tiny and quiet
exercise: A boy says, «Saw your mum at the food bank, ha!» What should Precious do? | Tell a teacher, it's bullying | Laugh along so it stops | Never go out with Mum again | Tell everyone all of his secrets back
exercise: Why is teasing back risky, even though it's understandable? | It can make the bullying worse | It always makes the bully stop | Teachers only punish the second one | Poundland is a very nice shop really
exercise: What are benefits? | Government money for people who can't work enough or care for someone | Prizes given to people who win quizzes and game shows on the telly | Money that families have to pay back to the bank | Extra pocket money given to kids by their schools
exercise: Why can't Megan's mum go out to work? | She looks after Megan's disabled brother all day | She doesn't want to work and likes the sofa | She's waiting to win the lottery next week | She's too busy going to the shops all day
exercise: What can Ruaridh share when the class talks about holidays? | What he did, like the park or the beach | A made-up trip to Florida with a pool | Nothing, he should stay quiet all lesson | That holidays are boring and pointless
exercise: The note says Everett's family owes $12 for lunches. Whose fault is it? | Not his, it's a family money matter | His, for eating too much at lunch | The teacher's, for writing the note | His friends', for taking his money
exercise: What should Everett do with the lunch note? | Give it to his mom and ask about free lunch | Throw it away and hope nobody asks again | Pay it from money borrowed from a friend | Stop eating lunch so the bill stops growing
exercise: Sami's dad says don't tell family business. What can Sami tell his teacher? | Just «No computer at home» | Everything about Dad's job | Nothing, even if it hurts his work | A lie that his computer broke
exercise: What did Tasha give the birthday girl? | A friendship bracelet she made | An expensive gold necklace from a shop | A box of shop chocolates | Nothing, she stayed home
exercise: Jamal's buddy paid for his hot chocolate. How can Jamal pay back without money? | A favour, a drawing, or being a good mate | He must pay back double next week | He must buy the next ten drinks | He can't, so he should avoid him
test: «my mates have all got Air Max, I'll just say mine are in the wash, easy». What will probably happen? | The fib will need more fibs and get tiring | The fib will work forever, nobody checks | Her mates will buy her the trainers | Her trainers will turn into Air Max
test: Jory comes to Reece's and sees the ripped sofa. What's most likely? | He doesn't care, he came to have fun | He tells the whole school about it the next day | He refuses to sit down for an hour | He goes home early to sit on his own sofa
test: Megan's classmate: «my dad says ppl on benefits r lazy». Which reply is calm and true? | «My mum cares for my brother. That's a full-time job.» | «Your dad's thick and so are you, shut up.» | «You're right, my mum is lazy, sorry.» | «My mum's a secret millionaire, actually.»
test: In a film, the poor kid lies about having a mansion and everyone loves him when the truth comes out. What's more real? | Lies get harder, and friends like you for you | The truth never comes out about any lie | A mansion lie always makes more friends | Everyone loves being lied to by friends
test: The teacher asks «where did u go» and three kids said Spain, two Florida, one Cornwall, and Ruaridh nowhere. How many children in this group went abroad? | Five | Six | Seven | Two
test: «Everett owes money lol, he's a thief». What's true? | It's his family's bill, not his fault | Yes, he stole $12 from the dinner lady | Yes, kids who owe money are always thieves | No, but he should pay it from pocket money
test: Precious: «that's twice this week, he does it every day». What's the best thing to do now? | Tell a teacher, it keeps happening | Wait for it to stop by itself | Bring a gang to sort him out | Stop going to school for a week
test: What would most likely happen if Jamal kept avoiding his buddy? | They'd drift apart over a hot chocolate | His buddy would send him a bill for it | His buddy would pay for even more drinks | Nothing, friends never notice avoiding
test: Tasha: «a bracelet's well cheap, she'll hate it». What happened in the end? | She wore it all day | She threw it away | She gave it back | She laughed at it
test: Lacey's coat was Keeley's; her jumper was her cousin Mia's; her shoes were from her big sister. A kid says, «Everything you've got is someone's old stuff, ha». What's the smartest answer? | «Yep, saves loads. Warm, innit?» | «It is not, I bought them all myself!» | «Shut it or I'll push you in a puddle» | «Your stuff is uglier than mine, so there»
test: Sami thinks: if I tell the teacher we've no computer, I'm telling all Dad's business. Is he right? | No, he can share just what he needs | Yes, so he should say nothing at all | Yes, so he should lie about it | No, he should tell her everything
test: Seven kids at the table, Ruaridh included: two went abroad, one went to Cornwall, the rest went nowhere. «Everyone goes away except me», he thinks. How many others stayed home like him? | Three | None | Four | Two
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the concealment of a family's poverty by falsehood? | It is wearisome, since each falsehood tends to require further falsehoods | It is effortless and is invariably sustained without any difficulty | It is required of every pupil by the regulations of the school | It reliably secures the friendship of the pupil's classmates
exam: Identify the principal purpose of state benefits as established in the lesson. | To support persons unable to work, poorly paid, or caring | To reward persons who decline employment without any reason whatever | To provide sums which must be repaid to a bank | To pay pupils for attendance at their schools
exam: Given that a pupil is repeatedly mocked by a classmate concerning his family's use of a food bank, ascertain the most appropriate course. | To report the matter to a teacher | To mock the classmate in return | To absent herself from school | To conceal the matter indefinitely
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a pupil whose family owes money for school meals. | The debt is a family matter, not the pupil's fault | The pupil must repay the debt from his own pocket money at once | The pupil is to be excluded from the dining hall | The debt is evidence of theft on the pupil's part
